Here's how to wire it up:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
public static class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
    {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

        // HttpClient + data service: one instance shared app-wide
        builder.Services.AddSingleton<HttpClient>();
        builder.Services.AddSingleton<IDataService, DataService>();

        // ViewModels: new instance each time they're requested
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainViewModel>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailViewModel>();

        // Pages: transient so each navigation gets a fresh page
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainPage>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailPage>();

        return builder.Build();
    }
}
```

**Why these lifetimes?**

| Registration | Lifetime | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `HttpClient` | Singleton | Reusing one instance avoids socket exhaustion; safe to share across threads. |
| `IDataService` | Singleton | Holds the `HttpClient` and any cache/state; one instance keeps data consistent app-wide. |
| ViewModels | Transient | Each page navigation should get fresh state (no stale data from a previous visit). |
| Pages | Transient | MAUI pages hold UI state; transient avoids showing leftover content on re-navigation. |

**Key takeaway:** services that manage shared resources or connections → **Singleton**. UI-bound objects (pages, view models) that should reset per navigation → **Transient**.

If a ViewModel were expensive to create but needed per-page state, you could use `AddScoped` inside a custom scope per navigation, but `Transient` is the standard MAUI pattern.
